<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<RelativeLayout xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
  android:id="@+id/relLayoutContactListViewElement"
  android:layout_width="match_parent"
  android:layout_height="wrap_content">

  <View
    android:id="@+id/e2ee_contact_element_divider_view"
    android:layout_width="wrap_content"
    android:layout_height="25dp" />

  <ImageButton
    android:id="@+id/e2ee_verify_contact_unverified_button"
    style="?attr/e2eeStripContactUnverifiedButtonStyle"
    android:layout_width="25dp"
    android:layout_height="25dp"
    android:layout_alignStart="@id/e2ee_contact_element_divider_view"
    android:layout_centerVertical="true"
    android:layout_marginStart="10dp" />

  <ImageButton
    android:id="@+id/e2ee_verify_contact_verified_button"
    style="?attr/e2eeStripContactVerifiedButtonStyle"
    android:layout_width="25dp"
    android:layout_height="25dp"
    android:layout_alignStart="@id/e2ee_contact_element_divider_view"
    android:layout_centerVertical="true"
    android:layout_marginStart="10dp" />

  <!--
    Both name views live in one container that is constrained to end before the tag.

    The previous attempt constrained only the LAST name view - and the last name is optional while
    the first name is mandatory, so the one field that had to be bounded was the one that wasn't.
    An unconstrained wrap_content child in a RelativeLayout is measured AT_MOST(row width), so a
    long first name spanned straight across the tag view; the tag is declared later so it drew on
    top, in the same colour, on identical ink. Measured, that erases the tag entirely on ordinary
    names - "Maria del Carmen Fernandez" among them, which is the name the length cap was relaxed
    to accommodate.

    Bounding the container is what makes the claim true for both fields at once.
  -->
  <LinearLayout
    android:id="@+id/e2ee_contact_name_container"
    android:layout_width="0dp"
    android:layout_height="wrap_content"
    android:layout_centerVertical="true"
    android:layout_marginStart="10dp"
    android:layout_marginEnd="5dp"
    android:layout_toEndOf="@id/e2ee_verify_contact_unverified_button"
    android:layout_toStartOf="@id/e2ee_contact_address_tag_element"
    android:orientation="horizontal">

    <!--
      Both name views are weighted, so neither can starve the other.

      They were plain wrap_content in a wrap_content container: the first child was measured against
      the whole bounded width, so a long first name left the last-name view at ZERO width - with no
      ellipsis and nothing on screen to say a name had been cut. Measured, "Maria del Carmen
      Fernandez"/"Smith" and the same first name with "Jones" rendered byte-identically, in plain
      ASCII with no Unicode involved. The comment below promised "the names are what get ellipsised";
      for the last name that was true only in the sense that it was truncated to nothing.

      Weights make the truncation visible in both, which is the property that was claimed.

      They are wrap_content rather than 0dp, which is the difference between weights distributing
      the LEFTOVER and weights dividing the whole column. At 0dp each view took exactly half
      whatever it held, so an empty last name - and the last name is the optional field - left half
      the column blank while the mandatory first name was cut in half. Measured at 320dp, "Maria
      del Carmen Fernandez" showed "Mari" with 61px sitting empty beside it.
    -->
    <TextView
      android:id="@+id/e2ee_contact_first_name_element"
      style="?attr/e2eeStripContactListNameFieldStyle"
      android:layout_width="wrap_content"
      android:layout_height="wrap_content"
      android:layout_weight="1"
      android:layout_marginVertical="5dp"
      android:layout_marginEnd="5dp"
      android:ellipsize="end"
      android:maxLines="1"
      android:textSize="20sp" />

    <TextView
      android:id="@+id/e2ee_contact_last_name_element"
      style="?attr/e2eeStripContactListNameFieldStyle"
      android:layout_width="wrap_content"
      android:layout_height="wrap_content"
      android:layout_weight="1"
      android:layout_marginVertical="5dp"
      android:layout_marginEnd="5dp"
      android:ellipsize="end"
      android:maxLines="1"
      android:textSize="20sp" />
  </LinearLayout>

  <!--
    The address tag has its own view, anchored to the END of the row.

    It used to be appended to the last-name TextView, which is wrap_content with only a start
    constraint - so a long enough name made the tag measure zero width and simply not render, with
    no ellipsis and nothing to say it was missing. An untagged row then reads as the plain original
    one, which is exactly the confusion the tag exists to prevent. Capping the name length did not
    fix it: the cap counts characters and the layout consumes width, so eleven EM SPACEs beat any
    character cap on any device.

    The margins around the row were 30dp a side and the tag 14sp. Measured, that left 74dp of name
    column on a 320dp screen - "Alice Smith" rendered as "Alice ..." - and nothing at all at font
    scale 2.0, where the column computed negative. Priority (tag over name) is right, but "a
    truncated name is visibly truncated" stops being true when there is no room for any of it.
    Trimmed to 10dp on the END side and the tag to 12sp. The START side was 30dp on the verify
    buttons, putting the name column's left edge at 65dp (30 + 25 + 10); that is now 10dp, so it
    begins at 45dp and the column is 20dp wider. Measured, that is what moves 320dp/fontScale 1.5
    from "Al"/"S" to the whole of "Alice Smith".

    Anchored here, and with BOTH name views inside a container bounded by it, the tag always
    renders and the names are what get ellipsised. That is the right way round: a truncated name is
    visibly truncated, a missing tag is invisible.
  -->
  <TextView
    android:id="@+id/e2ee_contact_address_tag_element"
    style="?attr/e2eeStripContactListNameFieldStyle"
    android:layout_width="wrap_content"
    android:layout_height="wrap_content"
    android:layout_centerVertical="true"
    android:layout_marginEnd="5dp"
    android:layout_toStartOf="@id/e2ee_contact_button_delete_contact"
    android:maxLines="1"
    android:textSize="12sp" />

  <ImageButton
    android:id="@+id/e2ee_contact_button_delete_contact"
    style="?attr/e2eeStripInputFieldRemoveButtonStyle"
    android:layout_width="25dp"
    android:layout_height="25dp"
    android:layout_alignEnd="@id/e2ee_contact_element_divider_view"
    android:layout_centerVertical="true"
    android:layout_marginEnd="10dp" />
</RelativeLayout>
